+# Research consulted
+
+- VMware VCF Upgrade Planner — https://vmware.github.io/vcf-upgrade-planner/ — consulted 2026-08-14. Its vSphere-to-VCF 9.1 path places existing VCF Operations and disaster-recovery products before the infrastructure conversion/upgrade, and post-infrastructure log management after ESX and Automation work.
+- VMware VCF Upgrade Planner compatibility data — https://vmware.github.io/vcf-upgrade-planner/compat-data.json — consulted 2026-08-14. The selected vCenter 8.0 U3g/U3h, ESX 8.0 U3g/U3h, NSX 4.2.2.2/4.2.3.1, Operations 8.18.5, Automation 8.18.1, Live Site Recovery 9.0.5.0, and vSphere Replication 9.0.5.0 combinations have paths to the chosen 9.1 target. The data does not offer an in-place Logs 8.18.3-to-9.1 path, so the architecture uses a sized replacement and forwarding cutover.
+- Broadcom Knowledge Base article 410435, “Unified VCF Product Releases & Versioning” — https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/410435/unified-vcf-product-releases-versioning.html — consulted 2026-08-14. VCF bill-of-materials components use the unified 9.x version convention, including vCenter, ESX, vSAN, NSX, Operations, Operations for Logs, Automation, vSphere Replication, and Site Recovery.
+- Broadcom Knowledge Base article 425089, “Required Component Binaries for VCF 9.1 upgrade” — https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/425089/required-component-binaries-for-vcf-91.html — consulted 2026-08-14. The primary VCF instance hosts fleet management components and therefore needs the full primary-instance management-services binary set.
+- vmware/vcf-api-specs tag 9.1.0.0 — https://github.com/vmware/vcf-api-specs/tree/9.1.0.0/specifications/vcf-installer — consulted 2026-08-14. The greenfield artifact follows the tag's `SddcSpec`, including its Operations, Automation, NSX, vSAN, network, and management-domain sections.
